/datum/book_entry/liezs_guide
	name = "Liezillith a La Lumiere d'Etoiles' Guide to Healing and Reviving"

/datum/book_entry/liez_guide/inner_book_html(mob/user)
	return {"
		<div>
		<h2>"There are none who cannot be saved, as long as the soul is earthbound, they can be brought back."</h2>

		<h2>A note from the Kingsfield College of Pestran Sciences:</h2>
		The contents inscribed upon this book are ones of an inflammatory nature upon Our Fine College, and incredibly unprofessional. In accordance with the wishes of the University of Kingsfield, however, this text will be made available in a form more curtailed to sensibilities befitting a prospective graduate.

		<h2>Introduction</h2>
		<i>Bonjour</i>, I am Liezillith <i>a La Lumiere d'Etoiles</i>, one of the best doctors in Psydonia and the bane of the Pestran College.<br>
		While Grim's guide focused on the basics, which I do recommend giving a quick read, this will cover advanced things as well. While this book has separated entries on different types of surgeries, these were compiled and written by the stuck ups at the Pestran College, <i>fait chier</i>, thus I'll go over everything here as well, out of spite.<br>
		That aside, this is a very comprehensive guide, and should contain everything one requires to know in regards to the medical field. This should assist you on everything related to the healing and revival of others.<br>
		</div>
		<br>

		<div>
		<h2>Preparation, tools, and how to use them</h2>
		Before beginning, make sure you have all your tools, and nothing is covering the injured areas. This means armor, clothes, or bandages. Having a bed, or a bedroll to lay them on is also incredibly useful for not messing up. Don't just suddenly stop to realize you forgot your speculum inside the previous patient. <i>Con comme une valise sans poignée</i>. <br>
		<br>
		<b>The Examination Hammer:</b> Unless you have formal training, this is what you use on any patient to check what wounds they have and what you need to treat. Do try not to smack people with it instead. Even if it is very tempting.<br>
		<br>
		<b>Needle:</b> Stitch up cuts and all types of wounds. They are also used in healing organs, and usually used first for any badly bleeding cuts. You can make one with a fiber and a thorn. If empty, remember to thread in more fiber. Try to do it more than once so you don't have to constantly do it as you try to stitch up a fleshy blood spewing pincushion.<br>
		<br>
		<b>Scalpel:</b> Used to make incisions to get access to wounds, though keep in mind cuts in general can be substituted for incisions. This is also the only procedure where failure still gives you what you want, although it causes a lot more bleeding. Can be replaced by any dagger, but remember to weakly cut into them, instead of stabbing your patient. If you are hurrying, sometimes stabbing them to cut them faster is acceptable.<br>
		<br>
		<b>Speculum:</b> Used so you can access organs and for some of the other treatments, it can be put in if the selected body part has a cut or an incision. Mostly used for the chest cavity and head, to spread the skin open to give you access.<br>
		<br>
		<b>Forceps:</b> They can be used to clamp bleeders when a speculum is inserted, so they can slow the bleeding. They are also the fastest tool to use for healing burns and bruises quickly. Forceps are also needed for turning a heart into a chimeric one, and for lobotomies.<br>
		<br>
		<b>Improvised speculum and forceps:</b> If you are missing speculums or forceps, you can make some from two sticks and a fiber. These are named improvised retractor for speculum, and improvised clamp for forceps.
		<br>
		<b>Saw:</b> Cut through bones, cut off limbs.<br>
		<br>
		<b>Bone Setter:</b> Sets bones when they are broken, or fixes dislocated limbs. You do not need to make any incisions for dislocations.<br>
		<br>
		<b>Cautery Iron:</b> Used to cure infections and rot, be it from the body or organs.<br>
		<br>
		<b>Leech/The Parasite:</b> Used to treat toxin of any kind, be careful for leaving them on too long, since they also start draining blood, especially if they get rid of all of the toxin. The Parasite is a better leech, and can also infuse blood. Squeeze it to have it swap between extracting and injecting.<br>
		<br>
		<b>Syringe:</b> Inject blood or medicine directly into people. Remember to adjust how much liquid you want to inject or take out, it is a maximum of 15 ligulae.<br>
		<br>
		<b>Cloth/Bandages:</b> Used to stop bleeding, buy you time to handle blood loss, and slowly heal bruises and burns. Do remember that if a wound is not treated it will start to bleed through the bandage, and a bloody bandage will not do anything. Strain and clean the blood before reapplying a bandage, bundling up with another bandage or cloth does this the fastest. Cloth can be made from using a needle on two fibers, while bandages are crafted by those decently well skilled in medicine. They are made with two cloth and two fiber. These are a pain to make, but they are better at absorbing blood.<br>
		<br>
		<b>Water:</b> Used to VERY slowly regenerate blood, better than nothing but pretty useless if the patient is dead.<br>
		<br>
		<b>Lifeblood/Other Healing Potions:</b> Used to heal blood, wounds and bones, rich lifeblood is the strongest and overall best, of the lower ones: lifeblood is the general one, decent at everything, elderblood wine is better at giving a target blood, mending is better at healing bones, and pale serum heals organs.<br>
		<br>
		<b>Miracles:</b> Miracles, which a doctor does not generally use, but you may encounter clergy who wish to "help" you. They can, in general, heal limbs and body parts, and are quite helpful in treating people, Abyssorite ones are the best at giving back blood, Pestran ones are very good as well, and Pestrans can also cure rot. To restore blood, it is require to target the chest with the miracle. However please remember that miracles can also mess up a doctor, since it also heals incisions. And I swear certain clergy... <i>Ne pas avoir la lumière à tous les étages.</i> Note: They will never heal organs fully and will end up scarred, though they heal by themselves slowly, you can restore them faster yourself.<br>
		<br>
		<br>
		These are all the possible tools you may have access to. You will get very familiar with them.<br>
		</div>
		<br>

		<div>
		<h2>Treating people and preventing death:</h2>
		Your first action should be identifying the wounds, in this case using the examination hammer to check the wounds and to identify which are the most urgent issues. If you can tell the wounds without needing it then that's even better.<br>
		<br>
		1. Cut arteries and anemia is what usually causes deaths, light and normal anemia are not that bad, but if it becomes very bad or extreme anemia then you are on a very short timer before they die. Quickly stitch up the arteries with a needle and any cuts you see, do note that sometimes cut arteries become bruised arteries which will still bleed, though it is slowed if the limb is bandaged, they should be treated but they are not as urgent.<br>
		<br>
		2. Toxin. 50 and under you slap a leech, at 100 you put two or three leeches, if you somehow find someone with 400 toxin, the maximum possible, throw as many leeches as you can on them or they will die, remember to give them blood with your syringe or lifeblood if you do this. Multiple leeches are something I <b>HIGHLY</b> recommend on higher toxin levels, they do end up draining some blood as well but if they are at insane levels of toxin it's an acceptable loss. Note: Giving a someone blood of a different species gives them a tiny bit of toxin, this is negligible.<br>
		<br>
		3. Infections and Rot. Caused by death, walking through dirty water with open wounds, or keeping on bloody bandages for too long. You will usually see infections, light ones are not as bad, but acute infections usually means they are about to rot and turn into a deadite, prioritize this since it's quite fast to treat.<br>
		<br>
		4. Embedded objects. Bad for you, take them out and stitch it up.<br>
		<br>
		5. Cuts, punctures, bites, burns and bruises. The most common ones. Cuts, punctures and bites have priority for stitching up with a needle, burns and bruises are generally done afterwards or left to heal naturally with a bandage on top. If you are skilled enough, however, you may do the advanced technique of fixing burns and bruises at the same time, which sometimes fixes the other types of wounds as well. Though arteries will still need stitching afterwards.<br>
		<br>
		6. Oxygen Damage. Caused by organ damage or after being choked out. Usually causes brain damage. Not too hard to fix, and making others who are not trained do it is recommended if you are in a rush.<br>
		<br>
		7. Fractures and broken bones. As bad as they are to have, you leave them for last due to them not being life threatening.<br>
		<br>
		8. Nutcrack/Eggcrack and Testicular/Ovarian Torsion. They cause a lot of pain and vomiting. Lifeblood or miracles, they cannot be treated otherwise.<br>
		</div>
		<br>

		<div>
		<h2>Medicine itself!</h2>

		The most basic treatment is stitching a patient directly with a needle and bandaging the wounds afterwards.<br>
		<br>

		Fixing bruises and burns: Simply make an incision or use a cut on the chest, followed by using the forceps, you will have three options, advanced: treat bruises and burns, treat bruises and treat burns, the latter two are easier and recommended for those of lower skill, while the advanced one is a lot faster. Be careful with this, since failing to fix them will cause the patient to gain cuts, which add up fast and may kill your patient. Try and get your skill to the level of a master or legendary doctor to use the advanced version more consistently without killing the one you are treating.<br>
		<br>
		Surgery itself has these as the usual steps: Incision, use the speculum to open them up, and then clamp the bleeders using forceps, though forceps are not always needed if you are fast enough. Some treatments are an exception.<br>
		<br>
		To have access to organs you need to have the speculum inside them, you gain access to the arteries, and how you heal bruised arteries. You also gain access to bones, you can set them with the bone setter or by hand, doing it by hand is a lot slower.<br>
		<br>
		The following require you to add forceps as well: Curing rot, breaking bones and cutting off limbs.<br>
		<br>
		You cure rot by using a hot cautery iron, you can heat it by clicking it on stationary candles, torches and the like, you can also use silver psycrosses or some of the other amulets that have silver in them.<br>
		<br>
		Bone relocation or dealing with dislocated bones: can be done by using the bone setter on the limb without opening it up, if you don't have a bone setter, you can twist a limb back into place, though it may take quite a while.<br>
		<br>
		Reattaching limbs: with a weak intent, use the limb on the missing zone.<br>
		<br>
		In regards to curing rot: Check the head afterwards in case it has an ooze inside, if it does, remove it.<br>
		<br>
		Fixing organs: Heavy damage or death will mess up organs badly, causing two types of damage, the usual that is treated with the needle, or it being inflamed, which is rot, they are treated the same as rot that affects the body, using a cautery iron or a proper amulet.<br>
		<br>
		Facial Reconstruction: Fixing up a really messed up face or giving them a new identity. Scalpel, speculum and scalpel again.<br>
		<br>
		Breaking bones: Scalpel, speculum, forceps and using the saw. You can also use the scalpel and similar items.<br>
		<br>
		Amputation: Same as above, however, you will need to break the bone first.<br>
		<br>
		Inserting teeth: Weakly, while aiming for the mouth, insert them inside. This can be done with piles of teeth.<br>
		<br>
		Extracting teeth: The surgical way is using forceps on the mouth... the faster way is to just punch them in the mouth repeatedly.<br>
		<br>
		Lux: Very important thing to know, unless you are a Feldsher or are a Pestran acolyte, you require permission and to be supervised while doing anything related to lux. It's incredibly heretical to sell it, snort it or make wine with it, regardless of certifications. Messing with lux requires a good reason even for those allowed, and will cause you to get murdered.<br>
		<br>
		Lux Extraction: Note: They have to be alive for this, and some races do not have lux, keep that in mind. Incision, speculum, forceps, saw open the ribcage, then use knife or an empty hand. Do note that this is the second hardest surgery you can do, so it can fail very often, if you don't have high skill and have the difficulties from not using a bed or doing self surgery, it will be basically impossible to accomplish.<br>
		<br>
		Lux Transfusion: As above with the open chest and ribcage, simply use the lux on it. This is, however, the hardest surgery skills wise and as above you can have a lot of difficulty doing it even at higher skill levels.<br>
		<br>
		Revival: There are three ways of revival, though one is usually not used.<br>
		<br>
		Lux revival: It is the same as transfusion but while they are dead.<br>
		<br>
		Anastasis: Astratan clergy and Priests are capable of using this miracle and require a cross, it also needs them to be somewhat fixed up and without too much organ damage.<br>
		<br>
		CPR: The one usually used. Simply target the chest while in weak stance. You WILL break the ribs, it can't be helped, if you have a speculum inside you can do it while being ready for combat. To have a successful CPR you need them to have low or no oxygen damage, minimal organ damage and some blood in them, anything except extreme anemia works, though it is recommended to get them to normal anemia at least. Remember to keep checking organs for damage, if they have been dead for a while they will keep decaying. Also remember to remove the ooze inside of their head or they will turn into deadites. Note: Compressions are a pain and the third hardest surgery, any non highly skilled doctors can fail this constantly and will need to take breaks to fix up the organ damage that was caused while they were screwing up the compressions. A doctor of legendary skill can cause revives to happen in a single compression, which are incredibly satisfying.<br>
		<br>
		Miscellaneous things:<br>
		Surgical dismemberment breaks bone but it's clean, someone getting their limbs chopped off will cause wounds that you need to stitch up, to do that you have to target the chest. Chopped off limbs from combat are weirdly intact.<br>
		Prosthetics can be detached by using a blade with a weak intent, and attached the same way you do a missing limb. They need engineering skill otherwise you cannot do it, it's currently in the works to change that however at the moment it doesn't work.<br>
		To shave someone use middle mouse button on the top of the head with a knife, shaving the beard is done by targeting the jaw and doing it twice.<br>
		You can throw normal leeches at people to attach them, but this is blocked by armor.<br>
		<br>
		The appendix is kind of useless, you can cut it out without anything bad happening, all it does is help handle toxins a little, but it usually just gets inflamed and causes toxin. The same can be said about humors, they do nothing and don't even need to be severed.<br>
		</div>
		<br>

		<div>
		<h2>How to revive someone in record time:</h2>
		Anyone who says someone is too mutilated to be revived is wrong. <i>Casse-toi.</i> As long as the soul is earthbound and they are not skeletonized they can be restored fully. (Note: You CAN revive a skeletonized person as long as the brain is there, but it's a tad more frowned upon and somewhat heretical)<br>
		You start with getting rid of rot and infections, make an incision on the chest and use the advanced treatment of bruises and burns, this usually deals with cuts as well, afterwards go in and manually heal the bruised arteries, followed by giving them blood if needed, if their friend or someone else is nearby make them do it, they don't need any medical knowledge for it, the same thing stands for giving them oxygen. Slap leeches on them once they have some blood if they have any toxin. Check the head organs first, remove the ooze and then heal them all up until it's only lightly damaged, "scarred and may be affecting its function" is a safe spot if you are too lazy to fix it to pristine, following this you move to the chest and heal up all the organs there, you can throw out the appendix and yoink some humors if you wish. Afterwards handle oxygen with mouth to mouth until it's low-ish, try 50s, this can be skipped if you had someone else do it, then you work on compressions. If you take too long you will have to continue checking organs from time to time since they will decay and the ooze in the head will reappear and start rotting them. Do note: the tongue is also an organ that may need healing, it usually doesn't but there are cases when it does. If you are a legendary doctor and are fast enough you can revive someone that resembles mush in a few minutes.<br>
		<br>
		Organ replacement notes: Sometimes it's a bit weird with eyes and you will have to take both out and put them back in if one is blind after transplanting it.<br>
		</div>
		<br>

		<div>
		<h2>Triage and mass casualties.</h2>
		Immediate ones are arterial bleeds, high infections and amputations leading to bleeding stumps. Rush and try to stop the bleeding and cure the rot, they are closest to death and you really want to avoid them dying.<br>
		<br>
		Urgent ones are anemic, very anemic or worse, with a lot of wounds that may slowly turn them anemic.<br>
		<br>
		Stable are all those without any bleeding wounds, minor bleeding, bruises, burns, fractures and paraplegia. Tetraplegia is technically stable but a bit more urgent.<br>
		<br>
		Dead. They're dead. Get to them afterwards, though with exceptions being other doctors or acolytes since they can assist you.<br>
		<br>
		Exceptions and changes: If you are at war or under attack, prioritize healers followed by important combat roles over others.<br>
		</div>
		<br>

		<div>
		<h2>Important things I forgot to write before</h2>
		To view the organs properly, drag the open flesh towards yourself, use a weak intention, as not to damage the incision more.<br>
		<br>
		You can treat infections by soaking a bandage in any alcohol.<br>
		<br>
		Soaking a bandage with lifeblood and wrapping it around a wound is INCREDIBLY effective. Though it consumes quite a lot of lifeblood.<br>
		<br>
		Remember, if the patient has friends or you have unskilled workers, put them to use! Make them feed the patients water, give them blood or oxygen via mouth to mouth<br>
		<br>
		People CAN in fact die from hunger and thirst... keep that in mind, both for patients and yourself. You are a doctor, if you're incredibly busy yell at others to bring you food.<br>
		</div>
		<br>

		<div>
		<h2>Humors</h2>
		Humors look complicated but are actually incredibly simple.<br>
		Remember: humors only work if the person with them is alive.<br>
		To work with humors you first need to prepare, open up the chest organ menu, before using forceps on the heart to turn it into a chimeric heart so it can store blood. You need an output and an input humor. Each of these require a semi random quantity of blood from a certain species, usually from the 35-45 range.<br>
		You can view active and inactive humors inside of a chimeric heart by using forceps on the heart, it will let you fiddle with the inserted humors.<br>
		<br>
		An input humor is the trigger, the output humor is the effect that happens. The blood required by humors is a <b>one time thing</b>. You can get the needed blood by having it injected or drinking it, you <b>can</b>, in fact, bite someone and drink their blood to get the needed type.<br>
		<br>
		You can modify humors by using the scalpel, forceps, speculum and then cautery iron to seal it. This will let you change them into certain other humors, of which, the one of note from this is the heartbeat humor.<br>
		<br>
		The most useful humors are:<br>
		Wounded that activates when you take any damage.<br>
		Restoring that heals burns and bruises whenever it's triggered.<br>
		Clotting that clots bleeding whenever activated.<br>
		Galewind that gives you a speed boost when activated.<br>
		Soft Landing negates fall damage and triggers a humor.<br>
		Pooling creates a <b>random</b> liquid, and it can be ANY liquid, from water, to milk, to death poison, to lux wine. <b>Anything.</b><br>
		Creation is the same except it directly injects it into your body. <b>This can instantly kill you and it's a massive gamble, be careful!</b> However there is a chance you will constantly have rich lifeblood injected into yourself, which makes you basically immortal.<br>
		<br>
		All the humors can be found in other sections, I won't bother listing all of them.<br>
		</div>
		<br>

		<div>
		<h2>I am not letting this person die, screw you I'm saving them.</h2>
		These are methods that will get you in trouble and can be considered heresy a lot of the time, though they are overlooked if it's a crisis or the person is important enough. Make sure to get permission otherwise... unless you don't mind dying for it or becoming outlawed or excommunicated. <i>C'est la vie</i><br>
		<br>
		Head transplant. Technically allowed but you need it to be the same species otherwise it is considered heresy. Cutting off the head and attaching it to another body and then reviving it. Note: If the body is a different race it may end up looking silly. Oddly, their physical characteristics remain the same, likewise for skills.<br>
		<br>
		Another possible way is a brain transplant, same as above except you simply replace the brain in one body with another. This can be done with any really messed up organ, and if you screw up with humors and need a new heart. Note: The same happens in regards to characteristics and skills as above.<br>
		<br>
		<h2>I cannot believe I am writing this but if you need a new heart, attach the second one first before cutting out the old one, or you will die instantly.</h2><br>
		</div>
		<br>

		<div>
		<h2>Extra organs, tails and other cosmetic effects.</h2>
		This is usually considered heresy, so expect to get in trouble for them.<br>
		<br>
		To add extra organs, if there seems to be no space, try fitting the organ between the others, you should be able to make more space.<br>
		<br>
		The best organs to get extras of are spleens and lungs, for more blood and oxygen respectively.<br>
		<br>
		Adding an extra heart will slowly starting killing you, but it takes quite some time.<br>
		<br>
		Replacing all your teeth with triton tusks will cause you to do a lot more damage by biting. You can also give yourself tails, plumage (that will make you cough goop) and beaks from medicators, and even harpy wings. Note: This is heresy.<br>
		</div>
		<br>

		<div>
		<h2>Brain damage</h2>
		Sometimes the brain gets permanently damaged, this causes... a lot of issues. Most obvious is them not being able to speak properly. Sometimes the only way to fix this is a lobotomy, which replaces the brain damage with more minor brain damage. Though if I am honest, it makes some people smarter. Somehow.<br>
		</div>
		<br>

		<div>
		<h2>Lobotomy</h2>
		Perhaps they have terrible brain damage, or someone is too much of an addict and the crown wants them "fixed", so you give them a lobotomy. That can be done by using forceps on the brain.<br>
		</div>
		<br>

		<div>
		<h2>Missing tools or cloth to cover bandages</h2>
		If a limb is very badly hurt and bleeding, grabbing and then aggressively grabbing it reduces the bleeding, giving you more time to get help or fix yourself.<br>
		</div>
		<br>

		<div>
		<h2>Splints and Tourniquets</h2>
		They are both improvised tools, splints are made with two sticks and two cloths, while tourniquets with two sticks and a cloth.<br>
		Tourniquets stop bleeding on wounds, alongside stopping feeling in them, but leaving them on too long will cause the limb to start rotting.<br>
		Note: You can in fact add tourniquets to both of your hands, but you will not be able to remove them and people will laugh at you. As you would deserve.<br>
		<br>
		Splints can be used on limbs with broken bones and they will let you use the limb even while the bone remains broken, good for temporarily fixing a limb so you can walk.<br>
		</div>
		<br>

		<div>
		<h2>Useful tools inside of the clinic</h2>
		The clinic inside Vanderlin has tourniquets and splints inside of the storage, in a chest. The storage has a ladder inside of it that leads to the operation theater, which requires the Feldsher's key to open. The most important thing inside of it is a locked chest that contains a dead rousman, the rousman is very useful for practice and increasing your medical skill. The chest also needs the Feldsher's key to open.<br>
		</div>
		<br>

		<div>
		<h2>Extra things I have missed:</h2>
		If an organ ever turns foul, you can fix that by cleaning it with a cloth.<br>
		</div>
		<br>

		<div>
		<h2>Deadite Infection! Festering Ooze!</h2>
		Once someone dies, or from being bitten and infected by a deadite, a festering ooze appears in the brain. This will cause you to slowly start rotting and turn deadite. For cure or revival you need to remove this. You can also implant it to infect someone! So have fun with that fun fact!<br>
		</div>
		<br>

		<div>
		<h2>GET OUT OF MY CLINIC, CUMBERSOME FOOL! <i>PUTAIN DE BORDEL DE MERDE!!</i></h2>
        Do not be afraid to tell people to get out if they are crowding the clinic, especially if they are getting in your way, this CAN get you in trouble but this is YOUR clinic. How rudely or how nicely you say it depends on you, but do not be afraid to tell people to stop getting in your way or crowding the clinic. <i>Je m'en fous</i>, the inn is next door.<br>
		</div>
	"}
